{% doc %}
  The promotional pill, account control and cart control.

  ## The cart count is Liquid, never JavaScript

  `sections/cart-drawer.liquid` names this section in its `data-sections`, so
  every cart mutation re-renders the header through the Section Rendering API
  and the number arrives already correct. Counting items in the browser as well
  gives two sources of truth that disagree the moment a discount removes a line,
  and the one the customer sees is the wrong one.

  ## The account control has three shapes, and Shopify owns the first

  **Account sheet** renders `<shopify-account>`, the component the Theme Store
  requires a theme to carry in its header. Shopify draws the avatar and the
  sheet that opens from it, and the sheet is what gives a signed-out customer
  Shop recognition, passwordless sign-in and the rest — none of which a theme
  can build for itself. It is the default for that reason.

  What the theme hands it is the menu handle and the signed-out avatar, through
  the `signed-out-avatar` slot, so the icon matches the other controls in the
  row rather than arriving as a second visual language. Everything past that
  belongs to Shopify, which updates the component independently of the theme.

  The one exception is *where the sheet lands*, which the component puts in the
  corner of the window rather than under the icon. `<account-anchor>` wraps it
  and moves it; the reasoning is in `assets/header.js`.

  **Theme menu** is the theme's own `<details>`, handled by the same
  `<nav-menu>` code as the main navigation so the animation, the hover intent
  and the Escape handling are not written twice. It opens on hover, which the
  account sheet does not.

  **Link** is a plain anchor to the login or account page — one target, one
  destination, nothing to expand.

  ## Nothing renders when the shop has no accounts

  All three are inside `shop.customer_accounts_enabled`. A shop with customer
  accounts turned off has no login page to send anyone to, so an account icon
  there is a control whose only outcome is a dead end — and the component's own
  documentation asks for the same check before it is rendered.

  @param {boolean} cart_is_drawer
  @param {string} cart_count_display - Already padded if the merchant asked
  @param {object} [section]

  @example
    {% render 'header-actions', cart_is_drawer: true, cart_count_display: '02' %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign st = section.settings
  assign show_labels = false
  if st.utility_style == 'text'
    assign show_labels = true
  endif
-%}

<div class="site-header__actions"{% unless st.promo_show_mobile %} data-promo-desktop-only{% endunless %}>
  {%- comment -%}
    One text, not two. The inner pill is the design and it stays exactly as it
    was; what changed is where its words come from.

    There used to be a Label setting *and* a Badge setting: the label sat outside
    the inner pill, in the outer pill's own colour, and was hidden by CSS below
    1400px - so on most screens a merchant typing into Label saw nothing happen.
    Badge did all the visible work. Label now feeds the pill, and Badge is gone.

    Still a link only when it points somewhere, and a static pill when it does
    not, rather than an anchor with `href="#"` that sends the customer back to
    the top of the page.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if st.promo_label != blank -%}
    {%- if st.promo_link != blank -%}
      <a class="site-header__promo" href="{{ st.promo_link }}">
    {%- else -%}
      <span class="site-header__promo">
    {%- endif -%}
      {% render 'icon', icon: 'tag', size: 14, class: 'site-header__promo-icon' %}
      <span class="site-header__promo-badge">{{ st.promo_label | escape }}</span>
    {%- if st.promo_link != blank -%}</a>{%- else -%}</span>{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Search sits with the other controls, immediately before the account button.

    It used to be slotted independently, which meant it could land on the far
    side of the navigation from the cart — three icon controls in two clusters
    with the menu between them. Customers reach for search, account and cart as
    one group; splitting them costs a scan of the whole row.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if st.show_search -%}
    <button
      type="button"
      class="site-header__action site-header__action--search"
      data-overlay-open="SearchDrawer"
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-controls="SearchDrawer"
      aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.open_search' | t }}"
    >
      {% render 'icon', icon: 'search', size: 20 %}
      {%- if show_labels -%}
        <span class="site-header__action-label">{{ 'general.search.title' | t }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </button>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if st.show_account and shop.customer_accounts_enabled -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign account_url = routes.account_login_url
      if customer
        assign account_url = routes.account_url
      endif

      assign account_style = st.account_style | default: 'sheet'

      comment
        The component takes a handle, not a link list. `customer-account-main-menu`
        is the menu Shopify's own account pages use, so it is what the setting
        falls back to when a merchant has not picked one.
      endcomment
      assign account_menu_handle = 'customer-account-main-menu'
      if st.account_menu != blank
        assign account_menu_handle = st.account_menu.handle
      endif
    -%}

    {%- if account_style == 'sheet' -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        `<account-anchor>` is `display: contents`, so it adds no box and the
        component stays a flex item of the actions row exactly as before. All it
        does is move the sheet: Shopify opens it as a modal `<dialog>`, which
        the top layer positions against the *viewport*, and the component's own
        placement is the top-right corner of the window — over the announcement
        bar, past the cart, and nowhere near the icon that opened it. The
        element measures the icon and points the sheet at it. See header.js.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <account-anchor>
      <shopify-account class="site-header__account-component" menu="{{ account_menu_handle }}">
        {%- comment -%}
          The slot, not `::part()`, for the glyph itself: a part can be styled
          but its contents still come from Shopify, and this is the theme's own
          account icon at the theme's own size. The wrapper carries the same
          class as the other controls so the circle, the hover and the sizing
          are the row's, not a second set written for one element.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span slot="signed-out-avatar" class="site-header__action site-header__action--account">
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'account', size: 18 %}
          {%- if show_labels -%}
            <span class="site-header__action-label">{{ 'sections.header.account' | t }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </span>
      </shopify-account>
      </account-anchor>
    {%- elsif account_style == 'menu' and st.account_menu != blank and st.account_menu.links.size > 0 -%}
      <nav-disclosure class="site-header__account">
      <details class="nav__details" data-nav-details data-kind="sub">
        <summary
          class="site-header__action site-header__action--account"
          data-nav-summary
          aria-expanded="false"
          aria-label="{{ 'sections.header.account' | t }}"
        >
          {% render 'icon', icon: 'account', size: 18 %}
          {%- if show_labels -%}
            <span class="site-header__action-label">{{ 'sections.header.account' | t }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </summary>

        <div class="nav__panel nav__panel--sub nav__panel--end" data-nav-panel>
          <div class="nav__panel-inner">
            <ul class="dropdown dropdown--sub" role="list">
              {%- for link in st.account_menu.links -%}
                <li class="dropdown__item">
                  <a class="dropdown__link" href="{{ link.url }}">{{ link.title }}</a>
                </li>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
      </nav-disclosure>
    {%- else -%}
      <a
        class="site-header__action site-header__action--account"
        href="{{ account_url }}"
        aria-label="{{ 'sections.header.account' | t }}"
      >
        {% render 'icon', icon: 'account', size: 18 %}
        {%- if show_labels -%}
          <span class="site-header__action-label">{{ 'sections.header.account' | t }}</span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  <a
    class="site-header__action site-header__action--cart"
    href="{{ routes.cart_url }}"
    {% if cart_is_drawer %}
      data-overlay-open="CartDrawer"
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-controls="CartDrawer"
    {% endif %}
    aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.open_cart' | t }}"
  >
    {% render 'icon', icon: 'cart', size: 18 %}
    <span class="site-header__cart-count" aria-hidden="true">{{ cart_count_display }}</span>
    <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'sections.header.cart_count' | t: count: cart.item_count }}</span>
  </a>
</div>
